  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1159 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nGulf moisture and the remnants of Hurricane Polo brought \nsignificant rainfall and subsequent flooding to eastern Nebraska\nand western Iowa last night. A swath of 3\"-5\" fell along I-80 \nfrom Lincoln to Omaha, with pockets of 6\"-8\". Lincoln's official\nweather station reached 6.15\", breaking it's all-time single-\nday rainfall record of 5.45\" set on Aug. 29th of 1910. Flooding \ncovered roads, submerged vehicles, and even triggered a mudslide\nnear Crescent, IA. Ongoing flooding of rivers and streams, is \nexpected to continue downstream into the Missouri River in \nsoutheast Nebraska. \n\nLingering spotty showers are expected to clear out this afternoon as \na surface high begins to build into the region behind the departing \nlow. Temperatures will stay in the mid to upper 60s with decreasing \nhumidity. Overnight, lows will fall into the mid 40s, before rising \nback into the 60s Friday with mostly sunny skies. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nHigh pressure will begin to build over the Four Corners region this \nweekend, with northwesterly upper level flow bringing cool dry air \nto the Central Plains. Highs will remain in the mid 60s to low 70s \nSaturday and Sunday. \n\nA trough will dip into the northeastern CONUS Monday, taking \ntemperatures down a few degrees, into the mid 60s. The over the \nintermountain west will shift eastward on Tuesday, bringing highs \nback into the 70s for Tuesday through the end of the work week. \nWhile we can't completely rule out a weak disturbance kicking up a \ncouple of showers around the middle of the week, overall moisture \nfinally looks to be lacking.",
